Myth: In the Silver Basin, water was the lifeblood of the civilization, yet it flowed only between two reservoirs managed by the Keepers of the Gate. When a Keeper opened the sluice, five measures of water would cascade into the lower basin, where the natural pressure of the mountain tripled the volume. This surge was a test of character: to retain the abundance was to risk stagnation, as the basin would eventually silt and choke, leaving the land dry for everyone. Instead, the Keepers established a covenant of balance. By returning exactly half of the multiplied flow to the source, they ensured the water remained clear and moving. This perpetual circulation transformed the desert into a lush sanctuary. They understood that wealth was not the water resting in the basin, but the current that flowed between them. If one Keeper acted with greed, the dance of life faltered; if they acted with fairness, the cycle sustained them indefinitely. In the Silver Basin, they proved that prosperity is not a static prize, but a living, breathing connection. Through their commitment to reciprocity, the Keepers ensured that their river would never run dry, forever mirroring the trust that kept their civilization thriving and vibrant.
